1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,385
Building with Orchard Core takes an editor, a few utilities, and a source of packages.

2
00:00:06,385 --> 00:00:11,303
This video covers the tools the documentation suggests, how to add the preview

3
00:00:11,303 --> 00:00:15,810
package feed, and how to build a local NuGet feed from the source code.

4
00:00:20,410 --> 00:00:24,801
You can build with Orchard Core in any tool that edits source files

5
00:00:24,801 --> 00:00:28,832
and builds a .NET application, on any platform .NET supports.

6
00:00:28,832 --> 00:00:35,750
The documentation names Visual Studio on Windows, the cross-platform Visual Studio Code, and JetBrains Rider.

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,742
For Visual Studio, the documentation suggests two optional extras:

8
00:00:40,742 --> 00:00:45,868
the Lombiq Orchard Visual Studio Extension, which adds utilities such as an error

9
00:00:45,868 --> 00:00:50,816
log watcher and a dependency injector, and code snippets from the Orchard Dojo

10
00:00:50,816 --> 00:00:53,880
Library for common module and theme scenarios.

11
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:59,355
DB Browser for SQLite opens the SQLite file of a tenant, found in

12
00:00:59,355 --> 00:01:03,215
App_Data, Sites, in a folder named after the tenant.

13
00:01:03,215 --> 00:01:06,410
By default, setup names it OrchardCore.db.

14
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:12,200
You can browse the tables, run queries, and display the JSON documents formatted.

15
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:17,239
smtp4dev is an SMTP server that runs locally to test email.

16
00:01:17,239 --> 00:01:22,583
Install it with dotnet, enable the SMTP Email Provider feature, and enter a

17
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:28,587
sender email address, host name and port number under Settings, Communication, Email.

18
00:01:28,587 --> 00:01:32,150
Its web interface lists the emails it processed.

19
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:41,705
Release packages are published to NuGet, built from the code of a release's version tag.

20
00:01:41,705 --> 00:01:47,517
Preview packages are published to Cloudsmith by a daily build, when the main branch has changes.

21
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:50,650
The Preview CI workflow can also be run manually.

22
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,062
Preview packages hold the development code:

23
00:01:54,062 --> 00:01:57,810
they are not the most stable, and can contain breaking changes.

24
00:01:57,810 --> 00:02:01,613
Use them to try a fix for a bug you reported, not in production.

25
00:02:01,613 --> 00:02:06,230
They are not kept forever, and nothing guarantees how long one stays available.

26
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:12,350
The preview feed is a NuGet source hosted on Cloudsmith, at the first address on screen.

27
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:17,606
It receives the packages of each successful preview build, and restoring packages from

28
00:02:17,606 --> 00:02:19,320
it needs no credentials.

29
00:02:20,070 --> 00:02:27,442
In Visual Studio, open Tools, NuGet Package Manager, Package Manager Settings, and select Package Sources.

30
00:02:27,442 --> 00:02:33,630
Add a source named OrchardCorePreview with the feed address, then choose Update, and OK.

31
00:02:34,380 --> 00:02:37,497
A NuGet.config file adds the same source.

32
00:02:37,497 --> 00:02:41,476
This one clears the inherited sources, then lists two:

33
00:02:41,476 --> 00:02:47,510
nuget.org under the key NuGet, and the preview feed under the key OrchardCorePreview.

34
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:51,233
The .NET CLI adds the source with one command:

35
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:55,808
dotnet nuget add source, followed by the feed address, and the name option

36
00:02:55,808 --> 00:02:59,010
with the name you choose, here OrchardCorePreview.

37
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:04,870
To install a preview package from Visual Studio, check Include prerelease in Manage

38
00:03:04,870 --> 00:03:08,889
NuGet packages, and select the preview source in Package Source.

39
00:03:08,889 --> 00:03:13,080
Preview versions carry the preview suffix, so they are prereleases.

40
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:20,550
To use a fork or a branch of Orchard Core in your own

41
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:24,485
site as packages, create a local NuGet feed from the source code.

42
00:03:24,485 --> 00:03:26,037
There are three steps:

43
00:03:26,037 --> 00:03:30,360
pack the packages, publish them to a feed, and point your project at it.

44
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:34,938
From the root folder of your fork or branch, run dotnet pack in

45
00:03:34,938 --> 00:03:38,822
the Release configuration, with an output folder of your choice.

46
00:03:38,822 --> 00:03:42,650
Every package of the source code is written to that one folder.

47
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:46,041
Next, publish the packages to a feed.

48
00:03:46,041 --> 00:03:50,463
The documentation uses a local feed, a folder on a server, and the

49
00:03:50,463 --> 00:03:55,930
nuget init command, which takes the output folder first and the feed folder second.

50
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:02,226
Then update your NuGet.config file so its package sources point to the feed.

51
00:04:02,226 --> 00:04:08,990
This one clears the inherited sources, then lists the local feed, named MyFeed, and nuget.org.

52
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:13,337
Each package takes the version of the source you packed.

53
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:20,009
OrchardCore.Application.Cms.Targets bundles the modules and themes as package references, so one reference to

54
00:04:20,009 --> 00:04:23,200
it restores them from your feed, at that version.

55
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:25,965
Reference that version everywhere.

56
00:04:25,965 --> 00:04:29,793
A package reference takes it in its Version attribute, and the CMS web

57
00:04:29,793 --> 00:04:34,427
application template takes it in its orchard-version option, so a project created from

58
00:04:34,427 --> 00:04:36,140
it references your packages.

59
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:42,358
Preview packages and local feeds let you build on the development code, or

60
00:04:42,358 --> 00:04:44,309
on your own copy of the source.

61
00:04:44,309 --> 00:04:45,680
Thanks for watching.

